%N A101349 Numbers of cubes between prime(n) and prime(n+1).
%C A101349 a(n) <= 1 for all n if and only if there is a prime between k^3 and (k+1)^3 for every k >= 1, which is an open conjecture. It has been verified by computation for k^3 <= 1.649*10^40 (Johnston, Sorenson, Thomas and Webster), so a(n) <= 1 whenever prime(n+1) <= 1.6*10^40; analytically it is known only for k >= exp(exp(32.537)) (Cully-Hugill). - _Pierre Colmant_, Oct 19 2026
%H A101349 Pierre Colmant, <a href="/A101349/b101349.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A101349 Daniel R. Johnston, Jonathan P. Sorenson, Simon N. Thomas and Jonathan E. Webster, <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.15564">Primes and almost primes between cubes</a>, arXiv:2601.15564 [math.NT], 2026.
%F A101349 a(n) = A048766(prime(n+1)-1) - A048766(prime(n)). - _Pierre Colmant_, Oct 19 2026
%e A101349 a(4) = 1 because 8 = 2^3 is the only cube between prime(4) = 7 and prime(5) = 11. - _Pierre Colmant_, Oct 20 2026
%t A101349 a[n_] := Floor[(Prime[n+1]-1)^(1/3)] - Floor[Prime[n]^(1/3)]; Array[a, 105] (* _Pierre Colmant_, Oct 20 2026 *)
%o A101349 (PARI) a(n) = sqrtnint(prime(n+1)-1, 3) - sqrtnint(prime(n), 3); \\ _Pierre Colmant_, Oct 19 2026
%Y A101349 Cf. A000040, A000578, A048766, A060199.
